        "title": "Colour blindness",
        "text": "Myths about the causes and symptoms of 'colour blindness' are common all over the world. The term itself is misleading, because it is extremely rare for anyone to have no colour perception at all. By looking into the myths about colour blindness, you can learn a lot about the structure and genetics of the human eye.\n\nIt is a myth that colour-blind people see the world as if it were a black-and-white film. There are very few cases of complete colour blindness. People who have no colour perception at all are called monochromats, and they usually have a serious problem with their vision in general as well as being unable to see colours. In fact, in most cases of colour blindness, there are only certain shades that a person cannot tell apart. These people are said to be dichromats. They may not be able to tell the difference between red and green, or between orange and yellow.\n\nA person with normal colour vision has what is called trichromatic vision. The difference between the three levels of colour perception is to do with the cones in the human eye. A normal human eye has three kinds of cone inside the retina: the red cone, the green cone and the blue cone. Each cone contains a particular pigment whose job is to absorb light of these colours and their combinations. People with trichromatic vision have all three cones in working order. When one of the three cones does not work properly, dichromatic vision is the result.\n\nSome people believe that only men can be colour-blind. This is also a myth, although it is not completely untrue. In an average population, 8% of males have some form of colour blindness, while only 0.5% of women do. There may be some truth in the idea that more men than women have trouble matching their clothes, but the reason that colour vision deficiency is mainly found in males has nothing to do with fashion. The fact is that the gene for colour blindness is on the X chromosome, and men have only one X chromosome. Women have two X chromosomes, and if one carries the faulty gene, the other one naturally makes up for it. So the only way for a woman to inherit colour blindness is for both of her X chromosomes to carry the faulty gene. This is why colour deficiency is sometimes more common in extremely small societies with a limited gene pool.\n\nIt is true that all babies are born colour-blind. A baby's cones do not begin to distinguish between many different colours until he is about four months old. This is why many modern toys for very young babies have black-and-white patterns or primary colours, rather than traditional soft pastels. However, some recent research points to the importance of developing a baby's colour vision. In 2004, the Japanese researcher Yoichi Sugita of the Neuroscience Research Institute carried out an experiment which suggests that colour vision deficiency is not entirely genetic. In his experiment, he kept a group of baby monkeys under monochromatic lighting for a year. He then compared their vision with that of normal monkeys that had experienced the colourful world outdoors. He found that the test monkeys could not do the colour-matching tasks that the normal monkeys could. Even so, most cases of colour blindness are put down to genetic factors that are present at birth.\n\nPart of the reason why there are so many contradictions about colour blindness, or 'colour vision deficiency' as doctors call it, is that it is difficult to know exactly which colours each person can see. Children are taught from a very young age that an apple is red. Naming colours allows children to link a certain shade with a certain name, whether or not they have a colour vision deficiency. Someone who never takes a colour test can go through life thinking that what they see as red is called green. Children are usually tested for colour blindness at about four years of age. The Ishihara Test is the most common, although it is strongly criticised because it requires children to be able to recognise numbers. In the Ishihara Test, a number made up of coloured dots is hidden inside a pattern of dots of a different shade. People with normal vision can pick out the number from the background, while those with a colour vision deficiency will only see the dots.\n\nAlthough modern science has disproved many of the myths about colour blindness, a few beliefs remain that need more research before they can be called folklore. For example, there is a long-standing belief that colour blindness can help soldiers, because it allows them to see through camouflage. Another belief is that everyone becomes colour-blind in an emergency. The idea behind this is that a catastrophic event can overwhelm the brain, so that it uses only the receptors needed for vital tasks. In general, recognising colour is not thought of as an essential task in a life-or-death situation."
